From: "adnan.drina" <adnan.drina@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 22:03:48 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 18/19] feat(guardrails): loop escalation also starts on
 same-result and same-call runs

RHOAI3 v32 (spring-petclinic-rest-legacy-v32-qwen38): two Qwen 3.8 loops
never produced the identical-call note, so patch 0017 never escalated them.
t_449a35e4 run 1 repeated one `ls | wc -l; date; ps aux` poll hundreds of
times (results differed only by the clock line; 62.8M input tokens, iteration
budget exhausted). t_2f2509aa run 30 issued ~141 calls of one pipeline
varying only `tail -c N`, every result with the same output_sha256.

Two optional start triggers, read from the same loop_escalation block:

- same_result_count: that many consecutive calls to one tool that are
  near-duplicates of each other (patch 0014's measure, _near_duplicates over
  _near_duplicate_call / _near_duplicate_arg_call, at the guard's configured
  similarity) and return a byte-identical result (patch 0015's reading,
  _result_content_lines: the terminal envelope's output without the
  runtime's own notices).
- same_call_count: that many consecutive calls with identical arguments,
  whatever the results.

Pollers exempt under is_stall_guard_repeatable neither count nor break a
run; an edit or verification call (classify_end_call), a background command,
an evidence-changing tool or a board transition restarts both counts, and so
does every start and end of an escalation. The executed call's raw result is
now passed to the escalation (_observe_loop_escalation). A start on one of
these triggers appends a factual note to the triggering result (the count,
and that the results were byte-identical or the call unchanged). The start
log line gains trigger=identical|same-result|same-call.

max_starts_per_signature bounds restarts: one stall signature (tool plus
argument hash, or tool plus result hash for same-result) may start that many
escalations in a run; the next start on it is a guardrail halt through the
existing halt path with the code loop_escalation_restart_halt.

Absent keys mean the 0017 behaviour (identical-call trigger only, unbounded).
The halting rules of 0001, 0012, 0014 and 0015 and the escalation's end
conditions, turn cap and request body are unchanged.

Tests replay the runs' kanban exec journal rows (tests/fixtures/
loop_escalation): same-call fires on the fifth unchanged poll (16:34:57Z),
same-result on the third `tail -c` call with an identical result; the restart
bound halts the third start; the Qwen 3.6 M1 capture sequence, process
polls, near-duplicates with differing results and patch 0015's paging cases
do not fire. Local extension.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
